WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:09.292
Welcome, Tom, to Microsoft Research. And it's great having you here

00:00:09.292 --> 00:00:14.497
as part of giving your distinguished lecture tomorrow at Microsoft Research AI.

00:00:14.497 --> 00:00:17.987
Just getting going about some of your background and interests, just

00:00:17.987 --> 00:00:20.154
curious how you got interested in machine learning to begin with.

00:00:20.154 --> 00:00:23.018
You started out in Oberlin. And were you always interested

00:00:23.018 --> 00:00:27.059
in mechanism and machines, or how did you get going into AI?

00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:30.201
Well, I guess I was always interested in kind of

00:00:30.201 --> 00:00:34.222
engineering things. I had an uncle who was an MIT Ph.D.,

00:00:34.222 --> 00:00:40.239
who worked in numerical controlled billing machines. And then he was one of the early people,

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:43.860
he was working for General Electric on the Dartmouth Time-Sharing System.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.654
So, as a young kid I got chance to write in basic on a Teletype,

00:00:51.553 --> 00:00:53.838
Interviewer: How did he end up giving you time on this machine,

00:00:53.838 --> 00:00:56.727
over at dinner?

00:00:56.727 --> 00:01:02.967
Yeah. But, then, so I was always doing computing.

00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:05.810
And in those days when you applied to college you said,

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.873
well, what computer do they have? Because they would have only one,

00:01:08.873 --> 00:01:17.192
right? So, Oberlin had an IBM 360 model 54 as well as a Ristas 11 PDP-8,

00:01:17.192 --> 00:01:26.763
or maybe it was PDP-11. So I really wasn't -- I considered many majors

00:01:26.763 --> 00:01:30.195
along the way. I was always taking a lot of math because that was fun.

00:01:30.195 --> 00:01:32.510
But one of the ones I considered was political science.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:37.538
And one of the classes in political science was philosophy of political science.

00:01:37.538 --> 00:01:41.868
And my professor instructed me to read Thomas Kuhn's "Structure of Scientific

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:46.079
Revolutions" because he said Karl Marx claimed that he was a scientist,

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:52.599
Marx did. And here's what Thomas Kuhn thinks science is. What do you think? Which was very

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.221
Interviewer: He had all these interesting ideas about big scientific paradigm shifts.

00:02:04.221 --> 00:02:08.628
Paradigm shifts, but the instrumentability of vocabulary and some of

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:12.289
subjectivism that maybe Marx also advocated.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: So,

00:02:12.289 --> 00:02:14.352
I'm getting lost now. How did this get into AI?

00:02:14.352 --> 00:02:22.187
In any case, when I decided to at least get a masters in computer science and I went to Illinois.

00:02:22.187 --> 00:02:25.647
And I was offered a research assistantship by Ryszard Michalski.

00:02:25.647 --> 00:02:30.892
And it was about what we now call machine learning.

00:02:30.892 --> 00:02:34.798
But in my spare time I was hanging out in the philosophy library,

00:02:34.798 --> 00:02:36.983
reading about the philosophy of science because I thought,

00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:39.667
well, if I'm going to be doing this machine learning stuff I should

00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:43.778
be trying to understand how is it that we learn about the world.

00:02:43.778 --> 00:02:46.472
And what are the sort of foundational principles.

00:02:46.472 --> 00:02:50.949
And so that led me into a lot of these kind of questions about

00:02:50.949 --> 00:02:53.675
Interviewer: Confirmation theory and Carnap.

00:02:53.675 --> 00:03:00.919
Carnap, yes. And then sort of, I don't know, through a connection with Donald Michie,

00:03:00.919 --> 00:03:05.788
I was introduced to Bruce Buchanan, who lo and behold, was a philosopher of science.

00:03:05.788 --> 00:03:11.036
And so then he offered me a research position as a graduate student at Stanford.

00:03:11.036 --> 00:03:16.903
And so these things kind of snowballed. But while I was still a masters student at Illinois,

00:03:16.903 --> 00:03:22.679
Tom Mitchell and Jaime Carbonell and Ryszard organized the first

00:03:22.679 --> 00:03:27.249
machine learning workshop which was at CMU in the summer of 1980.

00:03:27.249 --> 00:03:33.046
And all 20 of us or whatever were in the room. And so that was -- they decided to

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:38.114
revive the term that Arthur Samuel had chosen of machine learning as being the name

00:03:38.114 --> 00:03:41.058
Interviewer: I didn't realize that Arthur Samuel chose that phrase.

00:03:41.058 --> 00:03:44.509
Yeah, I think one of the titles in one of his first papers is some studies

00:03:44.509 --> 00:03:48.410
on the game of checkers or something like that.&gt;&gt; Interviewer:

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:53.490
Some of the early work in machine learning that I've read over the years really

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:58.628
seems to have laid out the whole architecture of modern machine learning quite early,

00:03:58.628 --> 00:04:02.559
like in the early '60s in fact. Some great work was done.

00:04:02.559 --> 00:04:10.698
Well, especially if you go to, say, Nils Nilsson had this book "Learning Machines," 1967 maybe.

00:04:10.698 --> 00:04:19.690
and Hart, I think, particularly. What a beautiful book they wrote.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: You mentioned that,

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:24.788
somewhere I saw you mention that you have research attention disorder.

00:04:24.788 --> 00:04:29.837
disorder, yes.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: Attention deficit disorder. I guess it's like,

00:04:29.837 --> 00:04:38.347
I think people that are the most fun in the field seem to be intellectually promiscuous. In other words,

00:04:38.347 --> 00:04:41.351
they're not going to dance around with attention deficit,

00:04:41.351 --> 00:04:44.723
but they go deep in several different areas over time,

00:04:44.723 --> 00:04:49.312
and they make links among those areas. And I always think that's kind of an interesting thing to,

00:04:49.312 --> 00:04:50.378
as a way to do science.

00:04:50.378 --> 00:04:55.851
I mean, I think you also maybe suffer from it.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: Or enjoy it.

00:04:55.851 --> 00:05:00.028
But, or enjoy it, yes. I find basically all of these,

00:05:00.028 --> 00:05:03.200
sort of every aspect of artificial intelligence is fascinating.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:10.893
And I've also always wanted to have one foot in some sort of an application setting, because, of course,

00:05:10.893 --> 00:05:16.271
we came of age kind of maybe in reaction to the artificial intelligence of

00:05:16.271 --> 00:05:22.248
1965 to 1975, which was all about the blocks world and kind of toy problems.

00:05:22.248 --> 00:05:26.358
And it was pretty clear that things that worked on those problems didn't really work on anything else.

00:05:26.358 --> 00:05:33.123
And I like to think of the history of artificial intelligence as the period of the

00:05:33.123 --> 00:05:37.074
known knowns when it was fully determined (indiscernible) observed systems.

00:05:37.074 --> 00:05:41.604
And then we had the known unknowns period of (indiscernible) and

00:05:41.604 --> 00:05:45.914
probabilistic reasoning over fully observable things more or less.

00:05:45.914 --> 00:05:52.255
And then we've moved now into the unknown knowns of how do we give systems that really work in open

00:05:52.255 --> 00:05:56.458
computer does not have a full model of what's going on.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: In fact,

00:05:56.458 --> 00:06:04.462
I think you've pointed out that having a full model in a world with, by definition,

00:06:04.462 --> 00:06:12.693
limited datasets is actually desired for the fundamental theorem of machine learning.

00:06:12.693 --> 00:06:18.237
Right. Because, yes, in machine learning we have learned

00:06:18.237 --> 00:06:21.263
that if we don't have enough data then we have to use a --

00:06:21.263 --> 00:06:24.845
models.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: You want to do that.

00:06:24.845 --> 00:06:31.221
Yes, in order to get the best, say, performance, classification, accuracy or predictive accuracy.

00:06:31.221 --> 00:06:35.381
And this is something that's deeply disturbing. If you've been raised kind of in

00:06:35.381 --> 00:06:38.933
the realist philosophy of science which says there's a real reality out there.

00:06:38.933 --> 00:06:45.219
The electrons really exist, for instance, even though we can't observe them, and our goal is to understand that.

00:06:45.219 --> 00:06:51.315
But if you come from the instrumentalist community, which is where Bruce Buchanan brought me up to be,

00:06:51.315 --> 00:06:57.980
we're really interested in sciences is important as it is useful to

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.313
we really care about.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: Now,

00:07:00.313 --> 00:07:05.263
you and I know, we've had deeply shared interests in AI and the open world and

00:07:05.263 --> 00:07:14.592
the implications of limitations in the technology in terms of influences on AI,

00:07:14.592 --> 00:07:18.823
people and society more broadly. And you've done some really fascinating

00:07:18.823 --> 00:07:24.490
review work and some deep dives into what it will take to build robust AI,

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:30.795
what we're up against in trying to achieve robustness in our AI systems as we field them in the open world.

00:07:30.795 --> 00:07:34.826
And you had a few ideas, like the idea of open category learning, for example.

00:07:34.826 --> 00:07:37.259
And just want you to make some comments on where you are with that.

00:07:37.259 --> 00:07:42.220
So, I mean, this began with an application problem. I was working

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:46.570
with ecologists and people at the Environmental Protection Agency,

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:51.149
and they wanted to assess the health of freshwater streams in the United States.

00:07:51.149 --> 00:07:55.340
And one way they do that is to sample the bugs that live in those streams.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:08:00.069
So it turns out if you have an insect larvae that may live for a month

00:08:00.069 --> 00:08:05.277
in a stream, it's in some sense drinking the water the whole time.

00:08:05.277 --> 00:08:10.445
And so if it's alive and healthy then that's a pretty good sign that the stream is healthy.

00:08:10.445 --> 00:08:15.494
So we had collected specimens -- well, our collaborators had collected 100

00:08:15.494 --> 00:08:21.211
specimens each of 54 different species of these kind of indicator insects,

00:08:21.211 --> 00:08:24.404
and we built a computer vision system that could automatically recognize them. We

00:08:24.404 --> 00:08:31.148
were very excited when it was 92 percent correct on 54 classes until we found out,

00:08:31.148 --> 00:08:34.311
well, if we were dealing with real field samples, they would

00:08:34.311 --> 00:08:37.015
contain a lot of other stuff besides our 54 species,

00:08:37.015 --> 00:08:42.133
because there are something like 2,000 species of insects that live in the rivers, not to mention

00:08:42.133 --> 00:08:48.649
insect-shaped leaves and little stones and things that might fool the computer vision system.

00:08:48.649 --> 00:08:56.062
And our system failed miserably when it was shown these -- we call them aliens now -- but these new,

00:08:56.062 --> 00:08:59.644
at least new to the computer, species of insects. So that

00:08:59.644 --> 00:09:03.704
really motivated me to think about this open category work.

00:09:08.133 --> 00:09:11.906
Can you describe for people watching this video what the open category challenge is?

00:09:11.906 --> 00:09:13.981
The open category challenge is that I've taught my

00:09:13.981 --> 00:09:17.533
computer to tell the difference among these 54 species,

00:09:17.533 --> 00:09:22.332
but someone at test time shows me a thing and says, what is it.

00:09:22.332 --> 00:09:25.844
And the standard way that we train a machine learning system,

00:09:25.844 --> 00:09:29.705
it really only knows, it thinks there are only 54 types of things in the

00:09:29.705 --> 00:09:33.776
universe. So, if you stick your thumb into the camera and photograph it,

00:09:33.776 --> 00:09:36.959
it would assign it to one of those 54 categories. Now it

00:09:36.959 --> 00:09:40.650
might say, well, I can't decide whether it's this or that.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:44.162
But it never enters its mind, so to speak, that there might

00:09:44.162 --> 00:09:46.697
be something else out there. And so the question is,

00:09:46.697 --> 00:09:51.745
how could we have a computer system that knows that it doesn't know everything? So it knows

00:09:51.745 --> 00:09:56.694
that it knows these 54 things but there are potentially thousands of other things out there.

00:09:56.694 --> 00:10:01.772
And so it entertains that explicitly as a possibility.

00:10:01.772 --> 00:10:06.601
And so our strategy has been to say for the computer --

00:10:06.601 --> 00:10:12.358
so when we train the computer system, the standard way is to ask it kind of like when training a

00:10:12.358 --> 00:10:17.646
physician: What are the key differences that would let you tell that this is a this versus that?

00:10:17.646 --> 00:10:24.042
If you ever tried to recognize bird species you know you're supposed to look for a red dot above the eye.

00:10:24.042 --> 00:10:29.898
Or if you're learning to tell poisonous mushrooms from delicious

00:10:29.898 --> 00:10:33.363
mushrooms, you maybe want to look for gills or something.

00:10:33.363 --> 00:10:35.915
And the same thing is true for these insects.

00:10:35.915 --> 00:10:41.103
But the end result is that the computer has learned to make these fine

00:10:41.103 --> 00:10:44.505
distinctions, but it doesn't really know what the whole insect looks like.

00:10:44.505 --> 00:10:48.496
It's just looking for that red dot and not for everything else. We

00:10:48.496 --> 00:10:52.846
instead want to have the computer learn models of that sort of,

00:10:52.846 --> 00:10:58.743
just give it a sense of what each of these species really looks like so that when it sees my thumb it says,

00:10:58.743 --> 00:11:01.107
well, that doesn't look like anything that I saw in my training

00:11:01.107 --> 00:11:06.146
data, so I refuse to assign it to one of my 54 categories,

00:11:06.146 --> 00:11:08.945
going to hand it to Eric here who is an expert and he'll tell me what it is.&gt;&gt;

00:11:08.945 --> 00:11:12.801
Interviewer: It's interesting to think about all the things we can do to give our

00:11:12.801 --> 00:11:16.652
systems either explicit or implicit knowledge that the world is bigger than they are,

00:11:16.652 --> 00:11:20.451
much, much bigger, and so they're ready to deal with being

00:11:20.451 --> 00:11:23.736
humble about what they know and what they don't know.

00:11:23.736 --> 00:11:31.000
I think you've also looked at this idea of how to do reinforcement

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.988
learning in a safe way or think deeply about missing exogenous variables,

00:11:35.988 --> 00:11:37.056
for example.

00:11:37.056 --> 00:11:43.792
Right. So that came up more in, not so much that the exogenous variables are missing,

00:11:47.233 --> 00:11:49.088
of course, because they're exogenous.

00:11:49.088 --> 00:11:53.888
So, I mean, this isn't really my own work, but I

00:11:53.888 --> 00:11:56.831
have looked at, as you say, I've been serving literature,

00:11:56.831 --> 00:12:01.822
and there's some very nice work by Simon Orr and his students where they -- one

00:12:01.822 --> 00:12:08.385
intuition I think we all have is that in situations where we know that we're ignorant,

00:12:08.385 --> 00:12:13.463
we should probably act more conservatively and carefully. And it turns out we can make

00:12:13.463 --> 00:12:21.425
this precise mathematically and show that if we minimize downside risk so that in,

00:12:21.425 --> 00:12:25.476
when we're training reinforcement learning systems we typically

00:12:25.476 --> 00:12:29.287
look at like the expected reward or the expected return.

00:12:29.287 --> 00:12:33.078
And you might be looking at that if you're investing in stocks on Wall Street, too. You might say,

00:12:33.078 --> 00:12:36.491
well, I want to choose a mutual fund that has a high

00:12:36.491 --> 00:12:39.983
expected return, and that's what they advertise in fact.

00:12:39.983 --> 00:12:44.493
But maybe really, particularly as you get older, you might be concerned about downside risk.

00:12:44.493 --> 00:12:49.830
You might say, I want to invest in a mutual fund that maybe it doesn't get the highest overall risk,

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:56.415
but the probability that I'll lose more than 50 percent of my funds, they like drive that near zero.

00:12:56.415 --> 00:13:01.015
And so it turns out that we can show that that kind of a notion that I

00:13:01.015 --> 00:13:08.598
want to limit my downside risk is equivalent to dealing with average,

00:13:08.598 --> 00:13:15.777
maximize my average return when I'm uncertain of the dynamics of the world. So this does confirm

00:13:15.777 --> 00:13:22.242
in a mathematical way that it's good to be conservative when you don't know how the world works.

00:13:22.242 --> 00:13:27.101
So you've recently been weighing in on this

00:13:27.101 --> 00:13:33.676
discussion which is evolving just maybe the last year or two.

00:13:33.676 --> 00:13:37.088
But it goes back a long ways about innate knowledge, innate

00:13:37.088 --> 00:13:42.596
structure, innate algorithms that intelligent systems might have,

00:13:42.596 --> 00:13:49.410
harkening back to human beings and going from toddlers into adulthood. What's

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:54.000
the innate abilities that we have and how do we learn on top of that?

00:13:54.000 --> 00:14:02.770
And there's various approaches to this. And I think you've been talking about your own perspective lately

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:11.260
or you at least raised questions about this in terms of this idea of a minimal system that we might build

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:17.124
some day that would do life long learning and become brilliant in the world and be able to do well.

00:14:17.124 --> 00:14:21.145
Any thoughts, your current thoughts on this discussion?

00:14:21.145 --> 00:14:30.108
So there are certainly, a significant portion of the AI research community and also, I think,

00:14:30.108 --> 00:14:34.158
the general public, when they think about artificial intelligence they think about something that would

00:14:34.158 --> 00:14:40.314
be an artificial human being or something that would have the same breadth of knowledge as people do,

00:14:40.314 --> 00:14:46.082
whereas our current AI systems are always extremely narrow. You cannot overemphasize how

00:14:46.082 --> 00:14:52.068
are.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: I call these things sort of brilliant savants.

00:14:52.068 --> 00:14:55.191
I don't throw the word "idiot" out too often, but narrow savants.

00:14:55.191 --> 00:15:03.641
They're very, very narrow. And that's a system we seem to know how to build right now.

00:15:03.641 --> 00:15:11.693
And so how does this connect to innateness? I guess we observe in people

00:15:11.693 --> 00:15:17.031
that they do come into the world with some degree of innate knowledge.

00:15:17.031 --> 00:15:24.597
We can't interview them to find out what it is. But they can do these studies on infants

00:15:24.597 --> 00:15:27.526
Interviewer: Certainly toddlers seem to have way more breadth even --

00:15:27.526 --> 00:15:31.249
Yes. And (indiscernible) systems can come close to a three-year-old in terms

00:15:33.154 --> 00:15:37.275
Yeah, that could be. So, it's not really my area of expertise. But

00:15:37.275 --> 00:15:41.835
certainly there are these studies and also studies on other animal species.

00:15:41.835 --> 00:15:47.242
So, we know many animals are born almost like adults. Obviously

00:15:47.242 --> 00:15:50.994
a horse can already stand and walk immediately after birth,

00:15:50.994 --> 00:15:55.783
whereas humans need an adult human to hang on for a year

00:15:55.783 --> 00:15:58.686
before they really are, even start to be able to walk.

00:15:58.686 --> 00:16:04.343
So one lesson you might take from that is one of the things that's

00:16:04.343 --> 00:16:08.483
allowed humans to be more broad is that we are born with less built-in,

00:16:08.483 --> 00:16:15.618
prewired knowledge. And if we want AI systems -- so this is

00:16:15.618 --> 00:16:19.180
kind of the paradox is that in order to get our narrow systems,

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:21.904
even our narrow systems to work we build in a ton of prior

00:16:21.904 --> 00:16:25.695
knowledge into them. There's a huge amount of innate knowledge,

00:16:25.695 --> 00:16:30.724
although it's often not explicit. I mean, innate knowledge

00:16:30.724 --> 00:16:32.789
is not like that they've memorized the times tables.

00:16:32.789 --> 00:16:37.019
It's more that there's some set of constraints so that when those constraints combine with

00:16:37.019 --> 00:16:42.058
experiences that the system has then it's able to rapidly do well in that narrow area.

00:16:42.058 --> 00:16:50.818
And one big thing that we would like to do is have our AI systems be broader. A lot of the

00:16:50.818 --> 00:16:56.405
mistakes that our AI systems make right now are because they are so narrow that they might --

00:16:56.405 --> 00:17:02.481
I mean, we saw this case recently of the -- I think it was BMW

00:17:02.481 --> 00:17:10.144
that had this self-driving car system that they had trained,

00:17:10.144 --> 00:17:14.155
and then they took it to Australia and it was being fooled by the motions

00:17:14.155 --> 00:17:19.253
of kangaroos. It had never really seen kangaroos hopping around before.

00:17:19.253 --> 00:17:25.579
And so that was an example that was just too narrow in one particular way and it couldn't deal with this broader thing.

00:17:25.579 --> 00:17:31.845
But also I think when we look at a lot of these ethical challenges of AI systems, where

00:17:31.845 --> 00:17:38.290
we're making misclassification errors that are really offensive to some people,

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:46.566
the computer is completely oblivious to the fact that some people might be insulted by being misclassified.

00:17:46.566 --> 00:17:51.719
Or it could be very insensitive, for instance, to be positioning advertisements

00:17:51.719 --> 00:17:57.007
for funeral homes opposite news stories about car crashes or something.

00:17:57.007 --> 00:18:01.513
Why would our customers be upset with that? It's because the computer system knows nothing about

00:18:07.022 --> 00:18:12.792
Yes, right. So, we'd definitely like our computer systems to be much broader. But to do

00:18:12.792 --> 00:18:19.924
that we need to be building in less or building in different kind of innate knowledge into them,

00:18:19.924 --> 00:18:25.483
because we know that if you try to learn starting with nothing, then it

00:18:25.483 --> 00:18:28.802
will basically take you an infinite amount of data to learn things.

00:18:28.802 --> 00:18:34.434
So there's a very tight tradeoff there between the amount of experience we need and the amount of

00:18:34.434 --> 00:18:39.551
to build into the system.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: You've been through many different,

00:18:39.551 --> 00:18:46.277
we'll call them dominant paradigms, back to Kuhn, of machine learning, going

00:18:46.277 --> 00:18:52.521
from certainty factors and heuristic methods to probabilistic ethical models,

00:18:52.521 --> 00:18:57.849
and now we're in this area of deep learning, these generalized

00:18:57.849 --> 00:19:00.773
function learning with differentiation and optimization.

00:19:00.773 --> 00:19:03.746
What is your sense with where things are going in terms of the

00:19:03.746 --> 00:19:07.003
rich semantics and the semantic foundations of learning systems?

00:19:07.003 --> 00:19:13.803
It's been quite a ride. And I think also there's been a -- the pendulum swings

00:19:13.803 --> 00:19:19.161
back and forth between whether we start with a formal understanding and then the experiments

00:19:19.161 --> 00:19:22.872
follow or we start with the experiments and then the formal understanding follows.

00:19:22.872 --> 00:19:27.716
And we're definitely in a period right now where there's this sort of crazy experimentation.

00:19:33.478 --> 00:19:37.929
Well, yes, I call it a giant hack-a-thon, like the whole field

00:19:37.929 --> 00:19:42.099
aided and abetted by the Internet and archiving and people just posting

00:19:42.099 --> 00:19:46.658
papers and software frameworks and it's been an incredibly productive time,

00:19:46.658 --> 00:19:52.794
I think, particularly surprising because we have all of these large corporate interests

00:19:55.907 --> 00:20:00.197
And very competitive terrain yet there's a tremendous sharing of

00:20:00.197 --> 00:20:04.548
the core tools and everybody's learning from everybody else very quickly.

00:20:04.548 --> 00:20:14.834
So, I mean, I think right now deep learning is getting a lot of attention, but in parallel --

00:20:14.834 --> 00:20:22.547
and why? Because it's given us an even more expressive set of functions or class of models,

00:20:22.547 --> 00:20:30.479
a language that lets us be able to use machine learning to do things we couldn't do before.

00:20:30.479 --> 00:20:36.026
And so that's -- and we -- and that really required, yes, coming up with

00:20:36.026 --> 00:20:39.348
the language, which we kind of had from neural networks in the '90s,

00:20:39.348 --> 00:20:43.319
but improved algorithms, much improved computation and vastly

00:20:43.319 --> 00:20:46.911
improved data and all those things really coming together.

00:20:46.911 --> 00:20:50.443
But those same factors are at work on the probabilistic modeling

00:20:50.443 --> 00:20:55.262
side. So you sort of left off with latent variable models,

00:20:55.262 --> 00:21:00.161
but then we had Bayesian nonparametrics, and now we have probabilistic programming.

00:21:00.161 --> 00:21:04.501
And interestingly we now have probabilistic programming systems that are compiling to

00:21:04.501 --> 00:21:09.330
the same TensorFlow infrastructure that the deep learning systems are compiling to.

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:15.646
And we're seeing lots of really interesting interplay between probabilistic reasoning, for instance,

00:21:15.646 --> 00:21:22.480
about say the variational autoencoders and high, high dimensional density

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:26.461
estimation. I think there's some extremely exciting ideas happening there.

00:21:26.461 --> 00:21:32.877
So this is using neural network type machinery but to do probabilistic

00:21:32.877 --> 00:21:38.025
modeling, because I think ultimately we need to be able --

00:21:38.025 --> 00:21:43.553
in order to understand and debug and ensure the safety of these AI

00:21:43.553 --> 00:21:46.576
systems, make sure they're actually doing what we want them to,

00:21:46.576 --> 00:21:51.694
we need able to understand what they've learned and the intermediate semantics.

00:21:51.694 --> 00:21:55.879
And I think the only tool we have for that comes out of probability,

00:21:55.879 --> 00:22:02.669
which gives us a declarative reading of what these variables mean, and it gives us the theory of identifiability

00:22:02.669 --> 00:22:08.466
of the parameters and all these things that we don't get from the pure function learning approach.

00:22:08.466 --> 00:22:10.847
On the other hand, the big lesson, I think, that we have

00:22:10.847 --> 00:22:13.325
to take away from the function learning was that in,

00:22:13.325 --> 00:22:19.890
in computer vision at least, right, we tried -- we know that to go from pixels all the way to saying,

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:26.146
oh, that is a person sucking on a lollypop or whatever, that is such a huge leap in

00:22:26.146 --> 00:22:33.669
interpretation that we needed to have some intermediate representations along the way.

00:22:33.669 --> 00:22:39.356
And we tried to hand-engineer those, and we had SIFT, our fellow graduate student, David Lowe,

00:22:39.356 --> 00:22:46.230
had invented this, and Paul Viola and HOG and so on. And it turned out these deep learning things

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:51.378
could just learn those representations directly instead much better than we could do them by hand.

00:22:51.378 --> 00:22:58.606
So we've learned that machine learning often can beat a human -- hand-engineering,

00:22:58.606 --> 00:23:06.626
but particularly in that sort of signal-to-symbol transition area where we just --

00:23:06.626 --> 00:23:14.805
we had no shortage of data, but we had this huge gap to cross. So

00:23:14.805 --> 00:23:18.277
I think we will see a lot more hybridization of the techniques,

00:23:18.277 --> 00:23:21.899
and I expect the higher level systems will look a lot more like probabilistic

00:23:21.899 --> 00:23:26.598
programming and even logical or relational type representations.

00:23:26.598 --> 00:23:32.655
But anything dealing with signal data, we now have this really powerful tool.

00:23:32.655 --> 00:23:41.295
And who knows, we can do end-to-end training of these systems now if we can make all the components differentialable.

00:23:41.295 --> 00:23:46.143
And several people are using this phrase differentialable programming to sort of describe what's going on,

00:23:46.143 --> 00:23:50.843
that these are not unformed neural networks; people really think very carefully about

00:23:50.843 --> 00:23:55.952
the ability of designing a skeletal program which is then filled in with data.

00:23:55.952 --> 00:24:01.180
A pipeline where you could actually literally have a surface to optimize over.

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:04.958
And we have memory units and we have time series units.

00:24:04.958 --> 00:24:09.940
All kinds of, again, innate things are being programmed in.

00:24:09.940 --> 00:24:16.086
And both in the probabilistic frameworks and in these deep learning frameworks we

00:24:16.086 --> 00:24:21.050
have much more expressive languages and ways of training and reasoning over them.

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:28.987
in Hamilton and Monte Carlo. This is just so cool.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: So, Tom,

00:24:28.987 --> 00:24:36.509
you and I were involved in the 2008, 2009 AAAI panel of Long Term AI

00:24:36.509 --> 00:24:41.099
Futures. And I think you actually cochaired one of the subcommittees,

00:24:41.099 --> 00:24:49.031
I think it was on short-term disruptions. And back when we had

00:24:49.031 --> 00:24:51.934
the meeting at Asilomar we had Andrew Ng get up and I asked him,

00:24:51.934 --> 00:24:56.394
I gave him a challenge, and I said, to kick off this whole day at

00:24:56.394 --> 00:25:03.069
Asilomar with this group thinking about of Long-Term AI Futures,

00:25:03.069 --> 00:25:08.547
tell us what's going to surprise us. And he got up and gave, essentially if you look back on his talk,

00:25:08.547 --> 00:25:13.855
he gave a deep learning talk, and that was in February of 2009.

00:25:13.855 --> 00:25:17.117
Interviewer: And that summer we had the kind of like the first breakthrough,

00:25:17.117 --> 00:25:21.866
I think, at Microsoft Research with speech. So if I asked you that again, now that we've

00:25:21.866 --> 00:25:28.791
been through a few surprises with the power of deepnet to do object recognition and so on.

00:25:28.791 --> 00:25:35.266
And I ask you this question now, what might surprise us, Tom, in the next five to ten years,

00:25:35.266 --> 00:25:36.852
what comes to mind?

00:25:36.852 --> 00:25:44.006
Well, one of the other things that we talked about in my group there at Asilomar was AI-enabled crime.

00:25:44.006 --> 00:25:49.244
And we've just seen a very interesting report come out across several

00:25:49.244 --> 00:25:56.697
organizations about potential misuses of the current AI technology.

00:25:56.697 --> 00:26:04.110
And it's clear there are risks in cybersecurity. We now have the ability to create extremely high

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:10.486
quality fake video in which you and I could be saying absolutely outrageous terrible things,

00:26:10.486 --> 00:26:17.769
which is sort of the automated fake news problem. So those, I'm

00:26:17.769 --> 00:26:21.371
afraid we might have some very bad surprises along those lines,

00:26:21.371 --> 00:26:26.780
because first of all we as people are kind of suckers for the interesting story

00:26:26.780 --> 00:26:32.469
and the conspiracy theory and we've seen that at play in social media.

00:26:32.469 --> 00:26:39.545
But also our institutions are not adapting as quickly as our algorithms are.

00:26:39.545 --> 00:26:44.244
At the same time, I guess, a reason for hope there is we are aware of this, and many people

00:26:44.244 --> 00:26:49.832
are scrambling to think about all of these ethical and social consequences of our systems.

00:26:49.832 --> 00:26:55.828
And the industry even more maybe than the universities is deeply engaged

00:26:55.828 --> 00:26:59.679
in this. And of course your leadership of the Partnership for AI,

00:26:59.679 --> 00:27:05.282
I think, is going to be a key part of preparing us to move forward

00:27:05.282 --> 00:27:07.442
Interviewer: How about technically though surprises?

00:27:07.442 --> 00:27:12.400
What might be an upside in the technical breakthrough?

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:17.781
Well, I would like to say that we would have some beautiful breakthroughs in, say,

00:27:17.781 --> 00:27:24.742
dialogue systems, that we could really have more than a one-step interaction with Cortana.

00:27:24.742 --> 00:27:31.577
And I think we'll see some progress there. But again it's going to have to be narrow

00:27:31.577 --> 00:27:37.144
because computer vision people like to say that anything can appear in an image.

00:27:37.144 --> 00:27:42.193
And natural language people can say anything, you can talk about anything in speech. In fact,

00:27:42.193 --> 00:27:45.924
unlike in images you can talk about abstract things -- ideas and trends and

00:27:45.924 --> 00:27:51.891
paradigms. And our computer systems don't have any idea what those are.

00:27:51.891 --> 00:27:56.081
And we don't know how to program those ourselves. And it's hard to

00:27:56.081 --> 00:27:58.446
figure out how we're going to get the training data for that either.

00:27:58.446 --> 00:28:08.493
We can't ground those the way we can with ImageNet or giant corpa. So I guess I'm not so optimistic,

00:28:08.493 --> 00:28:14.409
but I do think that we can have much better conversations than we have now.

00:28:14.409 --> 00:28:19.408
Another area that I think is going to be a huge opportunity is

00:28:19.408 --> 00:28:23.120
to couple deep learning with all these problems in medicine.

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:27.579
Everything in medical imaging is in the middle being totally revolutionized because

00:28:27.579 --> 00:28:32.149
these tools for deep learning that we've been using for entertainment on the Web are

00:28:32.149 --> 00:28:38.854
now being transferred to understanding all of these complex images in medicine.

00:28:38.854 --> 00:28:44.042
exciting, I think.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: Do you see implications for

00:28:44.042 --> 00:28:48.981
the nature of work and human jobs versus what the machines do and so on?

00:28:48.981 --> 00:28:51.163
What's your thinking about that?

00:28:51.163 --> 00:28:56.175
Certainly there are some work, including, let's say, my physician

00:28:56.175 --> 00:28:59.587
friends who describe their work as intellectual assembly line work.

00:28:59.587 --> 00:29:04.925
There are some aspects of many knowledge-worker type jobs that we'll be able

00:29:04.925 --> 00:29:09.654
to, I won't say automate necessarily but provide very good assistance.

00:29:09.654 --> 00:29:14.974
But I don't think that we're going to see entire jobs go away.

00:29:14.974 --> 00:29:21.609
I think jobs are going to be redefined so that some of the --

00:29:21.609 --> 00:29:29.734
what was currently on the job description will now be something we can do very effectively with an AI tool.

00:29:29.734 --> 00:29:38.782
And then other -- everything will rearrange as a result. And so I think

00:29:38.782 --> 00:29:43.018
virtually every job category will probably be affected by some of these things,

00:29:43.018 --> 00:29:48.136
just as the Internet and the Web, say, transform travel agents or

00:29:48.136 --> 00:29:56.288
many other things. And yet I think there's so many things where --

00:29:56.288 --> 00:29:59.690
anything that requires breadth, we know our computer systems

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:02.115
are going to continue to be narrow for a long time.

00:30:02.115 --> 00:30:04.649
And so I think any job that requires breadth and integration of

00:30:04.649 --> 00:30:09.608
information, dealing with humans and all of the human things,

00:30:09.608 --> 00:30:16.821
I think are areas that are going to be critical. Stuart Russell used

00:30:16.821 --> 00:30:20.947
to make this point in response to the skeptics about AI who would say,

00:30:20.947 --> 00:30:24.324
you're just making simulations. He would say, when a computer

00:30:24.324 --> 00:30:27.188
simulates playing chess, it's actually playing chess.

00:30:27.188 --> 00:30:32.406
And we now have, it plays Go as well. I think it's always going to be true when

00:30:32.406 --> 00:30:37.285
a computer simulates having emotions it's just simulating having emotions because

00:30:37.285 --> 00:30:42.403
not being human it can't have any direct experience of human emotion.

00:30:42.403 --> 00:30:47.182
So it's always just going to be struggling as an alien would struggle to understand us.

00:30:47.182 --> 00:30:53.169
And so I think all of the job functions that involve negotiation and understanding

00:30:53.169 --> 00:30:58.586
people and human experience are going to be very firmly in the place where humans

00:30:58.586 --> 00:31:01.599
those.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: The way I like to think about this,

00:31:01.599 --> 00:31:07.539
and I resonate deeply, is with the rise of automation around us, that

00:31:07.539 --> 00:31:17.982
there will be even an amplification and a celebration of human touch,

00:31:17.982 --> 00:31:23.779
human caring, maybe even the rise of a caring of human experience.

00:31:23.779 --> 00:31:28.628
So you mentioned earlier about you like to get into applications,

00:31:28.628 --> 00:31:35.393
not just theory via in the open world, both technically as well as making contributions there.

00:31:35.393 --> 00:31:39.952
And you've done incredible work in this rising area that you

00:31:39.952 --> 00:31:43.714
helped to define, we call computational sustainability now.

00:31:43.714 --> 00:31:50.339
Talk more about how you got involved in the whole biological world and environment and applications there.

00:31:50.339 --> 00:31:54.080
I think it's such an exciting and important space.

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:58.724
Yes, I guess I'd like to say it was a mid-career crisis,

00:31:58.724 --> 00:32:01.573
because after I got my Ph.D. and I got tenure and so on,

00:32:01.573 --> 00:32:07.649
and I was publishing lots of nice technical papers, but I didn't feel like

00:32:07.649 --> 00:32:11.321
I was having enough impact in terms of problems that really mattered.

00:32:11.321 --> 00:32:15.477
And one of those is the environment and the threats from climate change.

00:32:15.477 --> 00:32:24.012
And so being at Oregon State University, it turns out, there's always a toss up between Oregon State and

00:32:24.012 --> 00:32:32.283
as to who is number one in ecology.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: I missed out on that competition,

00:32:32.283 --> 00:32:33.351
but I believe it.

00:32:33.351 --> 00:32:40.227
It turns out like in forestry schools, ag schools. So they're

00:32:40.227 --> 00:32:44.376
tremendous, wonderful collaborators to work with at Oregon State.

00:32:44.376 --> 00:32:49.117
So I got involved gradually with several projects and we

00:32:49.117 --> 00:32:52.547
started a training program, a Ph.D. training program,

00:32:52.547 --> 00:32:55.720
in what we were calling at the time ecosystem informatics.

00:32:55.720 --> 00:32:57.945
Although informatics maybe wasn't quite the right word,

00:32:57.945 --> 00:33:03.872
because it was more simultaneously trying to support ecologist, ecological

00:33:03.872 --> 00:33:07.204
scientists who are trying to understand how ecosystems function,

00:33:07.204 --> 00:33:13.410
but also policymakers and, like the EPA or the forest service or farmers and

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:17.621
so on, who are actually managing an ecosystem trying to make it work.

00:33:17.621 --> 00:33:24.924
And so building tools, helping -- giving them, really taking the modeling tools that we have from,

00:33:24.924 --> 00:33:30.541
say, sequential decision-making and decision analysis and making it

00:33:30.541 --> 00:33:34.612
possible for them to apply them to these challenging problems.

00:33:34.612 --> 00:33:38.543
And so we also have, like, a summer training program and then we

00:33:38.543 --> 00:33:42.274
got these large grants from the National Science Foundation.

00:33:42.274 --> 00:33:48.051
And so then -- and one thing leads to another. So on the supporting ecological science side,

00:33:48.051 --> 00:33:53.130
I got heavily involved with collaborating with the Cornell lab of ornithology. And

00:33:53.130 --> 00:33:56.106
this brings us to another one of your favorite topics which is crowdsourcing.

00:33:56.106 --> 00:34:03.147
There are many passionate birdwatchers in the world. And Cornell was one of the first organizations

00:34:03.147 --> 00:34:08.116
to try to engage them in massive data collection efforts through something they called eBird,

00:34:08.116 --> 00:34:13.407
where birdwatchers can go out, go for a two-hour hike maybe and

00:34:13.407 --> 00:34:16.756
create a checklist of all the birds they saw along that hike.

00:34:16.756 --> 00:34:23.690
And then they can upload that to a website. And we promised we were going to do science with that data.

00:34:23.690 --> 00:34:29.824
But then it turned out to be tricky to do science with that data. And so I was one of many people

00:34:29.824 --> 00:34:36.202
working then on how to take that data that was being collected and figure out could we fit,

00:34:36.202 --> 00:34:39.913
could we understand bird habitat requirements, could we understand bird

00:34:39.913 --> 00:34:44.184
migration, which happens at continental scales with billions of birds.

00:34:44.184 --> 00:34:49.970
And so we've made some progress on those questions and it's been a load of fun.

00:34:49.970 --> 00:34:54.610
And then on the policy side, I was looking at problems, say, in

00:34:54.610 --> 00:34:58.890
wildfire management in the west or in invasive species management.

00:34:58.890 --> 00:35:03.609
And there you have tight budgets and you have to carefully choose what actions to perform.

00:35:03.609 --> 00:35:12.165
And you also have some serious risks -- massive fires or species extinctions or agricultural challenges.

00:35:12.165 --> 00:35:20.711
interesting problems to work on.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: So now that you have comma emeritus after your name,

00:35:20.711 --> 00:35:25.150
you might be in a position to give advice to folks who are just

00:35:25.150 --> 00:35:29.740
starting out in their careers about what to look out for,

00:35:29.740 --> 00:35:35.347
what to think about, what to reflect about, how to make decisions about a career in computer science.

00:35:35.347 --> 00:35:38.311
So do you have any thoughts you want to share with young computer

00:35:38.311 --> 00:35:41.723
scientists or people even thinking about computer science?

00:35:41.723 --> 00:35:45.015
Well, I think it's important to focus on the

00:35:45.015 --> 00:35:48.757
foundations, right. So, and that doesn't mean just taking,

00:35:48.757 --> 00:35:54.055
like, the foundational classes in computer science, but more -- there's a temptation, I think,

00:35:54.055 --> 00:35:57.451
and it's gotten very exaggerated lately in machine learning that

00:35:57.451 --> 00:36:01.079
we jump immediately to coding up an experiment and running it.

00:36:01.079 --> 00:36:09.839
And I think it's important to first consider what are the scientific hypotheses that you're testing.

00:36:09.839 --> 00:36:16.574
Too much of experimental computer science has turned into demos rather than into really asking what are

00:36:16.574 --> 00:36:24.456
the questions that we really need to answer in order to move our understanding of computing forward.

00:36:24.456 --> 00:36:29.155
And then what experiments can I do to answer those questions? Really taking a scientific approach to

00:36:29.155 --> 00:36:37.965
things rather than just trying to grab for the low-hanging fruit of whatever current paradigm is popular.

00:36:37.965 --> 00:36:45.089
And it's tough because reviewers at conferences may be looking for what's hot right

00:36:45.089 --> 00:36:48.092
now, and maybe they haven't thought about some of those other things for a while.

00:36:48.092 --> 00:36:57.021
But I think you need a rutter that's heading you toward success in the long term. And I think --

00:36:57.021 --> 00:37:00.653
and also part of that, I think, is keeping yourself honest by working

00:37:00.653 --> 00:37:06.899
on real problems. And so nowadays the secret is collaboration.

00:37:06.899 --> 00:37:11.519
You really need, particularly if you want to dive deep into a domain, you can't

00:37:11.519 --> 00:37:14.721
simultaneously be an expert in the domain and an expert in computer science,

00:37:14.721 --> 00:37:20.768
although you tried with an MD and Ph.D. But you really need to

00:37:20.768 --> 00:37:24.978
find a collaborator who can also do that deep dive with you.

00:37:24.978 --> 00:37:32.082
And they need to be willing to also learn some computer science. And you have to budget a

00:37:32.082 --> 00:37:37.729
year or two just to learn the vocabulary of each other's fields and understand also the,

00:37:37.729 --> 00:37:45.122
I say, social standards and morays, what counts as research result and how can you both succeed.

00:37:45.122 --> 00:37:52.084
But the payoff then is that you can not only discover new and important computer science questions,

00:37:52.084 --> 00:37:58.672
have an impact in those fields as well.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: You've been a fabulous advisor to so many students.

00:37:58.672 --> 00:38:03.302
Your Web page has a big list of all the successful students that have done Ph.D.s with you.

00:38:03.302 --> 00:38:05.795
Any advice for getting through a dissertation and the whole

00:38:05.795 --> 00:38:10.440
process, just more practical on the practical side?

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:17.628
Yes. Well, first of all, it's normal to feel despair at various points in the Ph.D.,

00:38:17.628 --> 00:38:24.682
and to feel that, I remember when I was stuck in my third year, which seemed to take two or three years,

00:38:24.682 --> 00:38:28.850
decide what would my thesis be about.&gt;&gt; Interviewer: I think I met you in those years.

00:38:28.850 --> 00:38:32.864
Yes, you did. I think I went through seven different topics

00:38:32.864 --> 00:38:38.431
or something. Everything either seemed impossible or too easy.

00:38:38.431 --> 00:38:47.429
And the lesson I learned was the "too easy" problems were also hard. So I think it's good to do --

00:38:47.429 --> 00:38:51.711
Donald Knuth has this advice, first solve a problem that will take

00:38:51.711 --> 00:38:54.794
you a minute, and then maybe one that will take you an hour,

00:38:54.794 --> 00:38:57.588
and one that will take you a day, and one that you think will

00:38:57.588 --> 00:39:00.162
take you a month, and one that ends up taking you a year,

00:39:00.162 --> 00:39:01.549
and then you graduate.

00:39:01.549 --> 00:39:11.436
So I think I have taught research methods and there's all these methods about how to do a statistical test correctly.

00:39:11.436 --> 00:39:15.746
But I think the number one question of research methodology is, what do I do today when

00:39:15.746 --> 00:39:19.937
I come into my office and sit down that is going to move me forward on this project?

00:39:19.937 --> 00:39:22.944
And as long as you do something every day that does that, you'll move

00:39:22.944 --> 00:39:26.083
Interviewer: And you probably won't feel so despaired.

00:39:26.083 --> 00:39:28.557
Well, yes, but you just have to pace yourself and

00:39:28.557 --> 00:39:31.620
realize that, yes, progress comes in small increments.

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:38.056
